From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 15:05:02 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] interner: checkpoint/truncate to rewind dynamically interned
 strings

The interner is append-only by design: Syms are bare indices, so
nothing can be evicted without knowing every holder. An embedder that
drops its ASTs after compilation, and whose compiled artifacts hold
JsStrings rather than Syms, knows when no dynamic Sym survives. For
that embedder the interner is dead weight between parse batches: it
retains every identifier from every script ever parsed.

checkpoint() snapshots the append state (span count, retired-slab
count, head length) and the per-string latin1_flags cache, which grows
in lockstep with the dynamic spans. truncate() rewinds to it.

Two details need care. Deduplication means a removed span's string can
shadow a surviving duplicate (the "" sentinel for utf16-only strings),
so the cache is rebuilt rather than having keys removed. Surviving
spans point into checkpoint-era slab memory, so a head that was later
retired into `full` is restored rather than freed.

Syms minted after the checkpoint dangle: resolve() returns None until
re-interning reassigns the index. Callers must hold no post-checkpoint
Sym, and the embedder has to enforce that structurally.
